  "api_error_status": null,
  "result": "The gate returned **OK** on the second of six allowed runs: all 7 witnesses gave the verdicts they expect.\n\n- **Run 1, `CHECK_FAIL`:** the units didn't match. The \"approaching\" test compared a velocity·distance product (m²/s) with a zero written in metres. I rewrote it as `vx·dx > −(vy·dy)`, which means the same thing but keeps one unit on both sides.\n- **Run 2, `OK`.**\n\n**The rule:**\n- **Who it applies to:** a vehicle, as defined in `DEF-NRS-484A-320::vehicle`.\n- **When it applies:** there is an intersection, or a railroad grade crossing, that the vehicle is either on or approaching. Approaching means its body is within `Units.feet 100` of the area and it is moving toward the area's centre point. The two kinds are checked separately with the same test. The rule does not apply while the vehicle is on a one-way roadway.\n- **The duty:** for every highway the vehicle is on, and every centre line on that highway, the vehicle's sideways offset from the centre line must not be negative. Negative means the vehicle is left of the line.\n\n**Choices:** I made no reading choices of my own, so there are no narrowings. The node's record already says what \"left side\" means and how \"driven\" is read.\n\n**Witnesses:**\n- The six planned ones were written as the node describes them.\n- In `branch_1`, the vehicle has already passed the crossing's centre point. That way, only being on the crossing (not approaching it) makes the rule apply.\n- I added `branch_3` (violated): a vehicle approaching a railroad crossing with no intersection in the scene. It tests the railroad version of the approach check, which no planned scene reached.\n\nOne command was blocked: my first shell call bundled `--help` and a directory listing with the gate command, which isn't allowed here. I read the Core files with the Read tool instead, so nothing was lost.\n\n`task_result.md` is in the working directory.",
